cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite the manuscript below."
title: "Measuring Aid for Learning: What Cross-Country Panel Evidence Can and Cannot Say"
authors:
  - family-names: Zurabishvili
    given-names: Shota
    email: shota.zurabishvili@gmail.com
    affiliation: "Independent researcher; Co-founder, Reed (reed.ge), Bristol, United Kingdom"
abstract: >
  A 133-country, 2010–2020 cross-country panel analysis of whether development
  assistance to education predicts learning outcomes, under a pre-analysis-plan-
  disciplined design in which every consequential analytical decision is fixed in
  writing before the analysis it governs is run. The within-country fixed-effects
  specification returns a positive aid-to-learning coefficient on the World Bank
  Harmonized Learning Outcomes measure (β = +11.14, p = 0.048, N = 143) that four
  pre-specified checks then break: the coefficient is sub-regional (carried by
  sub-Saharan Africa), measure-specific (sign-reverses on the Altinok-Angrist-
  Patrinos 2018 alternative), governance-specific (significant under only one of
  four pre-specified governance operationalisations), and sub-detectable in
  precision. Two further pre-specified commitments bite alongside these four.
keywords:
  - education aid
  - learning outcomes
  - cross-country panel
  - fixed effects
  - pre-analysis plan
  - methodological discipline
  - sub-Saharan Africa
